Answer: After the United Nations reform, the three core functions of the Emergency Relief Coordinator were:

(a) Policy development and coordination functions in support of the Secretary-General, ensuring that all humanitarian issues, including those falling between agency mandates such as protection and assistance for internally displaced persons, are addressed;

(b) Advocacy of humanitarian issues with political organs, notably the Security Council;

(c) Coordination of humanitarian emergency response on the ground, particularly through the Inter-Agency Standing Committee (IASC).

(Source: [P5], [P6])

Answer: After the United Nations reform, the three core functions of the Emergency Relief Coordinator were:

(a) Policy development and coordination functions in support of the Secretary-General, ensuring that all humanitarian issues—including those falling between agency mandates—are addressed;
(b) Advocacy of humanitarian issues with political organs, notably the Security Council;
(c) Coordination of humanitarian emergency responses on the ground, particularly through the Inter-Agency Standing Committee (IASC).

(Source: [P2], paragraphs 15 and 16.)

These functions comprise:

- Infectious diseases treatment and control;
- Trauma care;
- Primary care;
- Pediatric and geriatric care;
- Emergency care;
- Environmental health (including water and sanitation experts, food inspectors, vector control);
- Epidemiology;
- Ambulances and health transport;
- Pharmaceutical and medical equipment supply;
- Mental health and community mental health (including bereavement and psychological counseling);
- City, state, and national public health managers.

According to the assessment scales [P1/P2/P3], the following public health functions are required to routinely provide input to the city's disaster resilience governance mechanisms:

- Infectious diseases treatment and control;
- Trauma care;
- Primary care;
- Pediatric and geriatric care;
- Emergency care;
- Environmental health;
- Epidemiology;
- Vector control;
- Ambulances and health transport;
- Pharmaceutical and medical equipment supply;
- Water and sanitation;
- Food safety, cold storage, and distribution;
- Chemical and hazardous materials safety (where relevant);
- Mental health and community mental health, including bereavement and mental trauma counseling;
- City, state, and national public health managers.

